    "body": "Anne: Thanks for the comment. I think the warning will still make sense: my understanding for this step is the user agent can either \"abort the download\" or \"tweak the filename\", and the reason is given in the warning. However, the decision between the 2 options is unspecified and the warning is not bound to any specific option, so even we take out the aborting part, the warning still explains why it should ignore the suggested file name and instead \"let filename be set to the user's preferred file name or to a file name selected by the user agent\". Also the warning doesn't require the mentioning of \"cross-origin\" in this step, as it can be inferred from the steps before.\r\n\r\nI'm putting the aborting decision outside the algorithm as I feel the algorithm is mainly about getting the filename, but the aborting decision can possibly be made independent from / before running this algorithm. Also, the notion \"potentially hostile download\" is intended to be vague and contextless in order to cover browser specific criteria, and I feel it's better not to put it inside an algorithm which tends to be definite.\r\n\r\nPlease let me know if you are fine with this or how would you like it to be posed otherwise. Thanks!",
